---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import ColumnChart from '../../components/insights/ColumnChart.astro';
import DataNote from '../../components/insights/DataNote.astro';
import insights from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14.json';

const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : `${rawBase}/`;

const ctx = insights.contextWindows;
const yearly = ctx.yearly;
const half = ctx.series;
const y2024 = yearly.find((p) => p.period === '2024')!;
const y2026 = yearly.find((p) => p.period === '2026')!;
const latest = half[half.length - 1];
const num = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const pct = (a: number, b: number) => `${Math.round((a / b) * 100)}%`;
const short = (n: number) => (n >= 1_000_000 ? `${(n / 1_000_000).toFixed(n % 1_000_000 === 0 ? 0 : 2)}M` : `${Math.round(n / 1000)}k`);
// OpenAI's rule of thumb: 100 tokens is about 75 English words.
const words = (tokens: number) => num(Math.round((tokens * 0.75) / 1000) * 1000);
const snapDate = new Date(insights.snapshotDate).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
const medianInput = insights.priceSpread.medianInput;
---

<InsightArticle
  title="The typical new AI model now reads a million tokens"
  description="In 2024 the median new language model in our catalogue took in about 131,000 tokens. In 2026 it takes a million. The data, what a million tokens holds, and what the number does not tell you."
  date="2026-11-14"
  type="Analysis"
  readTime="7 min"
  snapshot={insights.snapshotDate} standfirst="In 2024 the middle-of-the-pack new model could take in about 131,000 tokens at once. In 2026 it takes a million. Most of that jump happened in the last six months."
>
  <p>
    Claude 3 Opus arrived in March 2024 with a 200,000-token context window, and that was large for the time. Of the {y2024.n} language models in our catalogue from 2024, {y2024.under200k} had a smaller one.
  </p>
  <p>
    This year looks nothing like that. We have added {y2026.n} language models so far in 2026 and {y2026.atLeast1M} of them
    accept a million tokens or more. The context window is the amount of text a model can hold in view at once: your
    question, any documents you attach, the conversation so far and its own answer.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat"><b>{num(y2024.median)}</b><span>median context window, models from 2024</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{num(y2026.median)}</b><span>median context window, models from 2026</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{ctx.yearlyGrowthMultiple.toFixed(1)}x</b><span>growth in two years</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{pct(latest.atLeast1M, latest.n)}</b><span>of models since July with a million tokens or more</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>The numbers, year by year</h2>
  <p>
    We took every language model in the catalogue that has a release date and a context window, removed duplicate entries for
    the same model, and found the median for each year. The median is the middle value: half the models are above it and half
    below. It suits this job better than an average, because one model with a four-million-token window would drag an average
    upwards on its own.
  </p>

  <ColumnChart
    title="Median context window of new language models, by year"
    summary={`The median rose from ${num(yearly[0].median)} tokens in 2024 to ${num(yearly[1].median)} in 2025 and ${num(yearly[2].median)} in 2026.`} data={yearly.map((p, i) => ({
      label: p.period === '2026' ? '2026 so far' : p.period,
      value: p.median,
      valueLabel: num(p.median),
      sub: `${p.n} models`,
      highlight: i === yearly.length - 1,
    }))} caption={`Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of ${snapDate}. Tokens. 2026 covers 1 January to 14 November.`}
  />

  <p>
    From 2024 to 2025 the median went from {num(yearly[0].median)} to {num(yearly[1].median)}, a rise of about half. From 2025
    to 2026 it went up five times over.
  </p>

  <h2>Slow, then sudden</h2>
  <p>
    Split the same models into half-years and you can see when it happened. For two years the median rose steadily. Then, between
    the first and second halves of 2026, it tripled.
  </p>

  <ColumnChart
    title="Median context window of new language models, by half-year"
    summary={`Half-year medians: ${half.map((p) => `${p.period} ${num(p.median)}`).join(', ')}.`} data={half.map((p, i) => ({
      label: p.period,
      value: p.median,
      valueLabel: short(p.median),
      sub: `${p.n} models`,
      highlight: i === half.length - 1,
    }))} caption={`Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of ${snapDate}. H1 is January to June, H2 is July to December. 2026 H2 runs to 14 November. The first group holds only ${half[0].n} models, so treat it with care.`}
  />

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Source: AI Resource Hub catalogue, snapshot of {snapDate}. "Under 200,000" and "a million or more" count
        models, not tokens.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Period</th>
          <th class="num">New models</th>
          <th class="num">Median window</th>
          <th class="num">Under 200,000</th>
          <th class="num">A million or more</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          half.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{p.period}</td>
              <td class="num">{p.n}</td>
              <td class="num">{num(p.median)}</td>
              <td class="num">{p.under200k} ({pct(p.under200k, p.n)})</td>
              <td class="num">{p.atLeast1M} ({pct(p.atLeast1M, p.n)})</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The last two columns show the change better than the median does. In the second half of 2024, 23 of 31 new models had
    a window under 200,000 tokens and one reached a million. Since July this year it is the other way round: 3 of 51 are under
    200,000 and 44 are at a million or more.
  </p>

  <h2>Who moved</h2>
  <p>
    Nearly everyone. Among the 51 models listed since July, every OpenAI and Anthropic entry sits at a million tokens or a
    shade over, as do six of Google's eight, six of Meta's seven and the new models from DeepSeek, Moonshot, MiniMax, Zhipu and
    Alibaba.
  </p>
  <p>
    There are seven exceptions. xAI's three Grok releases stop at 500,000. Two Gemini Omni Flash models and Meta's Muse Glimmer
    30B stay at 131,072, and NVIDIA's Nemotron 3.5 Lightning is at 262,144. Going by their names, those four are small
    or fast models, which is where you would expect a shorter window.
  </p>
  <p>
    One caution about that 44. OpenAI accounts for 16 of the 51 entries, because each of its models also has a Pro tier that
    we list separately. Leave OpenAI out altogether and the count is 28 of 35, which is still four in five.
  </p>

  <h2>What a million tokens holds</h2>
  <p>
    A token is a chunk of text, usually part of a word. OpenAI's <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/4936856-what-are-tokens-and-how-to-count-them" rel="noopener">guide to tokens</a> (read on 14 November 2026) gives the rule of thumb that 100 tokens is about 75 words of English. On that rule the 2024
    median of {num(y2024.median)} tokens is roughly {words(y2024.median)} words, and a million tokens is roughly {words(1_000_000)}.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the difference between attaching one long report and attaching a shelf of them. It is a rule of thumb for English
    prose, though. Code, tables and other languages split into tokens differently, so your own files may fit more or less than
    the rule suggests. Our <a href={`${base}token-counter/`}>token counter</a> will give you a real figure for a piece of text.
  </p>

  <h2>What the number does not tell you</h2>
  <p>
    The context window is a limit on how much a model will accept. It says nothing about how well the model uses what you
    give it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The best public evidence on that is a <a href="https://www.trychroma.com/research/context-rot" rel="noopener">July 2025 report from Chroma</a>, which tested 18
    models and found that "model performance degrades as input length increases", even on simple tasks. That report is older
    than all of this year's models, so it cannot tell us whether they have fixed the problem.
    We have not tested it ourselves and we do not have an independent source that has. Until one exists, treat a million-token
    window as room you can use, not a promise about quality when you fill it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Filling it also costs money, because you pay for every token you send, every time you send it. The median input price
    among the active models we track is ${medianInput.toFixed(2)} per million tokens, so one full million-token request costs
    about that much at the median before the model has written a word. At the top of the range, GPT-5.5 Pro lists at $30 per
    million input tokens. A long conversation resends its history on each turn, so those costs repeat.
  </p>

  <h2>If you are choosing a model</h2>
  <p>
    If you are choosing between the large models released this autumn, the context window will not separate them. Forty-four
    of the last 51 share almost the same figure. Price, speed and how well the model does your actual work are better things
    to compare, and our <a href={`${base}guides/choosing-an-ai-model/`}>guide to choosing a model</a> walks through them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The window still matters when you look at smaller, cheaper or older models, where 131,072 and 262,144 are common. Check
    the figure on the <a href={`${base}context-window/`}>context window page</a> before you plan a job around long documents.
    For how context windows work underneath, see <a href={`${base}blog/context-windows-explained/`}>our explainer</a>.
  </p>

  <DataNote anchor="calc-context-median" snapshot={`${snapDate} (catalogue rebuilt ${insights.cacheGeneratedAt.slice(11, 16)} UTC)`}>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>What we counted.</strong> {insights.coverage.withReleaseDateAndContext} language models with a release date and
        a context window, after removing {insights.coverage.llmEntries - insights.coverage.afterVariantRemoval} duplicate
        entries (dated snapshots and fast tiers of a model already listed).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Checked against the source.</strong> On 14 November 2026 we compared our figures with OpenRouter's live public
        list. Of 184 models we could match by name, 183 had the identical context window. The one that differed was Kimi K2.5.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Release dates.</strong> For most models the date is the day OpenRouter first listed it, which is usually close
        to the launch but not always. A few dates in the 2024 groups look wrong by some months, which is why the yearly chart
        is the safer comparison.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Coverage.</strong> This is the set of models we track, not every model released. It is strongest for the large
        labs and for models sold through an API.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </DataNote>
</InsightArticle>
